[interviewer · BRAINSTORM · 6:16] They then applied that to old COGS of 42% of revenue to reach 58%. But that scaling treats revenue as flat, while revenue grew 15%. The conversion needs a check, but the unit flag here concerns the shares of COGS, which they've handled with consistent bases. The cleaner issue is the 42% to 58% step. Walk me through the step from 42% to 58%. Does multiplying old COGS as a share of revenue by the input inflation figure account for the 15% revenue growth?

[candidate · 7:46] Fair challenge — let me redo it on a per-unit basis rather than on totals.

COGS as a share of revenue is really (unit cost × units) ÷ (price × units). The units cancel, so volume growth drops out entirely — the ratio only moves if unit cost or price moves. Unit cost is up 38%, price is flat, so the ratio goes 42% × 1.38 ÷ 1.00 = 58%. The 15% volume growth doesn't affect it.

Sanity check on totals: if revenue index goes 100 → 115, old COGS at 42% would be 42 → 48.3 at constant unit cost, then × 1.38 = 66.7. And 66.7 ÷ 115 = 58%. Same answer, so it holds either way.

Where volume does matter is absolute profit dollars, not margin — more units at a thinner spread.
